<article class="container pt-10 pb-5">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <h1 class="pb-3">Proxmox cluster</h1>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">What runs on the cluster</h2>
            <p>
                My three-node Proxmox VE cluster runs a mix of virtual machines and Linux containers.
                Home Assistant runs in its own VM.
                The cluster also hosts media services, Frigate for camera recording and detection, and workstation VMs for development.
                I keep workstation maintenance separate from routine updates to the services the household relies on.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Storage and hardware dependencies</h2>
            <p>
                Some guests depend on hardware attached to a particular host, such as USB radios for Home Assistant or a tuner for television recording.
                The NAS provides shared storage for services that need it, while Frigate uses dedicated recording storage.
                These dependencies influence where a guest can run and which services need to come back first after maintenance.
            </p>

            <h2 class="h4 mt-4">Backups and maintenance</h2>
            <p>
                I use Proxmox Backup Server for guest backups and keep separate copies of important service and host configuration.
                I update one node at a time, check quorum and guest health after each reboot, and exercise the services themselves.
                For example, a short television recording checks the tuner, the guest and the storage path together.
                Backup coverage varies by dataset, and restore testing is a separate part of recovery planning.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</article>
